import Mathlib
import TauCeti.Algebra.AlgebraicGroup.Representation.Tannaka.GroupFunctor

/-!
# Admissible pairs and p-adic Hodge structures: representative target signatures

The mathematical roadmap is `AdmissiblePairsAndPadicHodgeStructures.md`. This file records
definitions and theorem signatures statable against the pinned APIs and is not exhaustive.
It fixes the inner-twist order, the opposite adjoint-weight bounds and the empty-intersection
convention. The period sheaves, relative curves, Tannakian categories and moduli diamonds in
the roadmap require their actual geometric carriers before their signatures can be stated.
-/

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.AdmissiblePairsAndPadicHodgeStructures

open Set

universe u v

/-! ## Layer AP0: bilattices, trace filtrations and goodness

For `L₂ = t^a L₁` the first trace jump is `-a`. These exponents are integers, even though
the isocrystal slopes and Hodge weights used in the later layers can be rational.
-/

/-- Negating integral lattice exponents exchanges the two one-sided adjoint bounds. -/
theorem opposite_weight_bound (s : Set ℤ) :
    (∀ w ∈ s, w ≤ 1) ↔ ∀ w ∈ s, -1 ≤ -w := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer AP1: non-reductive isocrystals and basic bundles -/

section InnerTwist

variable {G : Type u} [Group G]

/-- The descent automorphism of the basic inner form is `Int(b) ∘ σ`, in that order. -/
def sigmaTwist (σ : G ≃* G) (b : G) : G ≃* G :=
  σ.trans (MulAut.conj b)

/-- Changing a Frobenius frame conjugates `b σ` to `g b σ(g)⁻¹ σ`. -/
theorem sigma_conjugacy_change (σ : G ≃* G) (b g x : G) :
    (g * b * (σ g)⁻¹) * σ (g * x) = g * (b * σ x) := by
  sorry

/-- Under the inner-twisted Frobenius, inversion gives the inverse modification datum. -/
theorem inverse_twist_identity (σ : G ≃* G) (b x : G) :
    b⁻¹ * sigmaTwist σ b x = σ x * b⁻¹ := by
  sorry

/-- Trivial basic datum leaves coefficient Frobenius unchanged. -/
example (σ : G ≃* G) (x : G) : sigmaTwist σ 1 x = σ x := by
  sorry

/-- An inner twist is trivial for a commutative group, including a torus’s geometric points. -/
example {T : Type v} [CommGroup T] (σ : T ≃* T) (b x : T) :
    sigmaTwist σ b x = σ x := by
  sorry

end InnerTwist

/-! ## Layer AP2: pointwise structures and their Tannakian groups -/

-- The concrete Hopf-algebra reconstruction is already present. The general neutral
-- Tannakian theorem is a separate supplier contract in the mathematical roadmap.
#check TauCeti.Tannaka.pointsFunctorIsoTensorAutFunctor
#check WittVector.Isocrystal
#check WittVector.isocrystal_classification

section TateSlope

open scoped Isocrystal

variable (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] [CharP k p]

/-- The Tate pair of index `n` uses isocrystal slope `-n`, so its Hodge weight is `-2*n`. -/
example (n : ℤ) (x : WittVector.StandardOneDimIsocrystal p k (-n)) :
    (WittVector.Isocrystal.frobenius
      (V := WittVector.StandardOneDimIsocrystal p k (-n)) p k).toFun x =
      (p : FractionRing (WittVector p k)) ^ (-n) •
        WittVector.FractionRing.frobeniusRingHom p k x := by
  sorry

end TateSlope

/-! ## Layer AP3: good reduction, Galois images and CM points

The two-lattice criterion retains the two rational structures. An étale lattice is analytic
relative to its isocrystal; the de Rham lattice is analytic relative to its étale realization.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP4: relative bilattices and flat bundles

The family on a disc with second lattice generated by `e₁, e₂ + (z/ξ)e₁` has a type jump at
zero. Its geometric lattice construction requires the relative period sheaves.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP5: Grassmannians for connected linear groups

The BB isomorphism uses the upper bound alone. The lower bound belongs to the inverse type.
-/

/-- A two-sided bound is precisely the conjunction of its two one-sided bounds. -/
theorem two_sided_weight_bound (s : Set ℤ) :
    (∀ w ∈ s, -1 ≤ w ∧ w ≤ 1) ↔
      (∀ w ∈ s, w ≤ 1) ∧ (∀ w ∈ s, -1 ≤ w) := by
  sorry

/-- Symmetric root weights make the upper bound equivalent to the lower bound. -/
theorem symmetric_weight_bounds (s : Set ℤ) (hs : ∀ w, w ∈ s ↔ -w ∈ s) :
    (∀ w ∈ s, w ≤ 1) ↔ ∀ w ∈ s, -1 ≤ w := by
  sorry

/-- The mirabolic weights in a cubic restriction of scalars separate the two conditions. -/
example : (∀ w ∈ ({0, 1, -2} : Set ℤ), w ≤ 1) ∧
    ¬ (∀ w ∈ ({0, 1, -2} : Set ℤ), -1 ≤ w) := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer AP6: neutral families and admissible loci

A neutral G-family requires each geometric fibre functor to have the standard form;
these fibre identifications are not extra choices in the object.
The rigid-base type theorem retains an independent full-rank de Rham criterion over
arbitrary p-adic fields as an open input. Finite-Q_p-field rigidity and a theorem whose
Levi-type hypothesis is already fixed do not establish that criterion.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP7: neutral Hodge structures and period domains

The universal Hodge family has weight homomorphism `2ν`. Geometric connectedness of its
domain is established after the duality in AP9.
A family-level inverse from Hodge structures to neutral admissible pairs requires a
globally constant isocrystal realization with a chosen frame; pointwise classification
does not trivialize its frame torsor.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP8: Hodge loci and special admissible subspaces

The full defining-line Hodge locus can have several fibre-functor forms. Its neutral piece,
rather than the entire Hodge locus, is the subgroup-admissible space.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP9: infinite-level spaces and basic duality

Inversion of a modification and the basic inner twist exchange the two period torsors.
For minuscule ambient type the three notions of special subvariety agree, including for
non-reductive subgroups.
-/

/-! ## Layer AP10: Hodge-generic points and exceptional loci -/

#check dense_iInter_of_isOpen
#check nonempty_interior_of_iUnion_of_closed

/-- The topological step in generic-point density: a countable union of closed sets with
empty interior has dense complement in a Baire space. -/
theorem dense_complement_exceptional_union {X : Type u} [TopologicalSpace X] [BaireSpace X]
    (E : ℕ → Set X) (hclosed : ∀ n, IsClosed (E n))
    (hinterior : ∀ n, interior (E n) = ∅) : Dense ((⋃ n, E n)ᶜ) := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer AP11: bi-analytic Ax–Lindemann and period-image density -/

/-- The analytic closure convention takes an empty family of containing level subspaces
to the whole ambient space. This is the exceptional case in the general theorem. -/
example {X : Type u} : ⋂₀ (∅ : Set (Set X)) = Set.univ := by
  sorry

/-- Each defining containing subspace bounds the intersection from above. -/
example {X : Type u} (F : Set (Set X)) (Y : Set X) (hY : Y ∈ F) : ⋂₀ F ⊆ Y := by
  sorry

/-- Enlarging the set being closed reduces its family of containing subspaces and enlarges
the resulting intersection; no finite-union axiom is used. -/
theorem containing_intersection_monotone {X : Type u} (C : Set (Set X))
    {A B : Set X} (hAB : A ⊆ B) :
    ⋂₀ {Z ∈ C | A ⊆ Z} ⊆ ⋂₀ {Z ∈ C | B ⊆ Z} := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer AP12: variations and Shimura applications

Variations with étale monodromy, the crystalline-frame cover, the Katz–Igusa example and
the arithmetic quotient in basic uniformization retain their full geometric statements
in the roadmap. The local special points concern the p-divisible group’s Q_p-structure.
-/

end TauCetiRoadmap.AdmissiblePairsAndPadicHodgeStructures
